course: everyday
lesson: 219
title: Fire, fireworks, garden dangers and the bathroom
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Matches and lighters are tools for grown-ups, because fires start fast; if you find them, you give them to a grown-up.
explain: In a fire you leave everything, even your tablet or your pet, and get out; you tell the firefighters about the pet, because people die going back inside.
explain: Fireworks and sparklers burn very hot, so you watch fireworks from far away, never pick up a used one, and put a finished sparkler into a bucket of water.
explain: Lawnmowers throw stones and can cut off toes, and small children can drown in very shallow water, like a pond or a paddling pool.
explain: A girl in Manchester leaves the fridge open while she decides on a snack; her dad explains that the fridge warms up and must work harder, so now she decides first.
fact: If you find matches or a lighter, you do not touch them and you give them to a grown-up.
fact: In a fire you leave everything and get out, and tell firefighters about any pets inside.
fact: You never go back into a burning building.
fact: You watch fireworks from a safe distance and never pick up a used firework.
fact: Sparklers are held with gloves at arm's length and put into a bucket of water when they finish.
fact: Stay well away from a lawnmower, even when it is off; mowing is a grown-up's job.
fact: Young children can drown in shallow water, so nobody plays near a pond or pool alone and paddling pools are emptied after use.
fact: When someone at the door is selling things, you politely say no and close the door, or get a grown-up.
fact: You do not lean or sit on hot radiators and heaters, because they can burn skin.
fact: Deciding what you want before opening the fridge stops it warming up.
fact: Putting the toilet seat down and closing the lid is polite and stops toddlers falling in.
fact: In a steamy bathroom you turn on the fan and open the door after, because damp grows mould.
exercise: You find a lighter on the kitchen table. What do you do? | Leave it and give it to a grown-up | Click it just once to see whether it works | Hide it in your bedroom | Take it outside to try it safely
exercise: The smoke alarm goes off and there is smoke in the hall. Your tablet is in your room. What do you do? | Leave it and get out at once | Run over and grab it really quickly | Go back for it once you are outside | Stop to switch it off first yourself
exercise: Your cat is still inside during a fire. What do you do? | Get out and tell the firefighters about the cat | Go back in quickly to find the cat, she knows your voice | Wait inside until you find her | Call her from the burning hallway
exercise: Why must you never go back into a burning house? | People die going back inside | Firefighters will be cross with you | The house will be locked up for good | Your things will already be safe
exercise: It is the Fourth of July and fireworks are going off. Where should you be? | Watching from a safe distance | Next to the fireworks to see better | Running to fetch the used ones | Holding one ready for the grown-up
exercise: A sparkler has just gone out. What do you do with it? | Put it into a bucket of water | Drop it on the grass | Give it to a little child | Touch the tip to see if it is cool
exercise: Why should you never pick up a used firework? | It can still be very hot or go off | It is somebody else's property and they may want it | It is dirty and smells | It might be needed again
exercise: Dad is mowing the lawn. Where should you be? | Well away from the mower | Close by, to help push it along | Picking up stones in front of it | Sitting on the grass he is cutting
exercise: The lawnmower is switched off. Can you play near its blades? | No, you never go near the blades | Yes, once it is quiet | Yes, if you wear shoes | Yes, if you only touch them gently
exercise: The paddling pool still has water in it after the party. What should happen? | It is emptied after use | It is left there until tomorrow | It is covered with a towel | It is left for the toddlers to play in
exercise: A man at the door wants you to buy cleaning sponges. What do you do? | Politely say no and close the door, or get a grown-up | Let him in to show you more of the things he is selling | Give him your parents' money | Tell him when your parents will be back
exercise: It is cold and you want to sit on the radiator. What do you do? | Do not sit or lean on it | Sit on it with shorts on instead | Lean on it for a long time | Sit on it only if it hums
exercise: You are hungry and stand with the fridge door open. What is better? | Decide what you want, then open it | Keep it open until you finally choose something | Open it many times quickly | Leave it open to cool the kitchen
exercise: You leave the toilet with the seat up. What should you do? | Put it down and close the lid | Leave it up for the next person | Close the bathroom door only | Flush again and leave
exercise: Your bathroom has no window and is full of steam after a shower. What do you do? | Turn on the fan and open the door after | Close the door tightly to keep all the steam inside | Leave it to dry on its own | Spray more water to clear it
test: A boy in Auckland sees matches in the living room and his little brother reaching for them. What is best? | Take them out of reach and give them to a grown-up | Show his brother how to strike one | Let his brother play with them | Hide them under the sofa
test: Why do firefighters prefer you to tell them about a pet rather than go back in? | They have gear to go in, and going back in kills people | Pets always escape by themselves | They do not like people inside | Going back in is only dangerous at night
test: On Bonfire Night in London, a girl wants to hold a lit sparkler. What makes it safer? | Gloves, arm's length, then into a bucket of water | Holding it close to her face so she can see the sparks well | Waving it near her friends | Dropping it on the lawn when it ends
test: Why should children stay away from a lawnmower in use? | It can throw stones and cut toes | It is too loud for children's ears only | It only cuts grass, so it is safe | It makes the grass slippery to walk on
test: A garden in Florida has a fish pond. What is the safe rule for a toddler? | Never near the pond without a grown-up watching | Only near it in the daytime | Fine if the pond is shallow | Fine if he can see the fish
test: Which visitor at the door could be a scam? | A stranger selling things you did not ask for | Your grandma bringing round a cake she baked for you | A neighbour returning your ball | A postman with your parcel
test: Why is a fridge door left open a waste? | The fridge warms up and must work harder | The light bulb inside gets tired and burns out | Food gets heavier when warm | It makes the freezer bigger
test: Why should the toilet lid be closed in a house with a toddler? | A toddler could fall in | The water will turn cold | It stops the toilet from running | Toilets break if left open
test: What can happen if a steamy bathroom is never aired? | Mould can grow from the damp | The mirror will crack from the heat | The towels shrink | The water gets colder
test: sparkler's done, where do i put it | In a bucket of water | On the grass | Back in the box | Give it to a little child
test: mower's off, can we play near it | No, stay away from it even when off | Yes, it's switched off so it can't do anything | Yes, sit on it | Yes, play with the blade
test: paddling pool's still full, we're done for the day | Empty it, small children can drown in shallow water | Leave it for tomorrow | Cover it with a towel | Put a toy in it
test: dunno what i want, gonna stand with the fridge open and look | Decide first, so the fridge doesn't warm up | Keep it open as long as you like, fridges don't care | Open the freezer too | Stand inside it
explain: A boy in Ohio lit a firework and went back to it when it did not go off; it exploded in his hand and he lost two fingers.
explain: A girl in Wales left a candle burning by the curtains and went to bed; the curtains caught fire, and only the smoke alarm woke the family in time to get out.
fact: Every year thousands of people are hurt by fireworks, most often their hands, eyes and faces.
fact: Smoke alarms give a family the few minutes needed to get out, because a house fire can fill rooms with deadly smoke in minutes.
test: rocket didnt go off, gonna go check it | No, never go back to a firework that didn't go off | Yes, relight it quickly | Yes, kick it over | Only after 10 seconds
test: Why must a candle never be left burning when you leave the room? | It can set things on fire while nobody is watching | Candles go out by themselves | It wastes the candle | Smoke alarms put candles out
test: smoke alarm going off, dunno why | Get out fast, stay out and call the emergency number | Hide under the bed | Look for where the fire is | Grab your toys first
test: did u hear a boy went back in for his Xbox when his house was on fire and he got trapped? real lesson? | Get out and never go back into a burning building | Grab only small, valuable things on the way out, then go | Go back if the fire is in a different room | Wait inside until the firefighters come
test: apparently a kid picked up a used firework and it went off and blew his hand clean off, my mate saw it. what's likely exaggerated vs true? | Details may be stretched, but never pick up a used firework | It's all made up; fireworks are safe | It's all true and only rockets do that | Used fireworks are safe after a minute
test: my nan's neighbour's smoke alarm went off at 3am and they all got out in pyjamas, the whole kitchen burnt. what's the real lesson? | Smoke alarms give a family the minutes to get out | Smoke alarms are only annoying when someone is cooking | Take the battery out so it doesn't beep | Fires only happen in the kitchen
exam: Identify the proper course of action upon finding matches or a lighter. | They are not touched but given to an adult | They are tested with care to see whether they work | They are hidden in a drawer | They are thrown in the bin at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises conduct during a fire in the home? | All belongings are left and one leaves at once, never returning | Valuables are collected before departure | One returns for pets oneself | One hides in a cupboard to await rescue
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice concerning sparklers. | They are held with gloves at arm's length and placed in water when finished | They are held close to the face to be seen | They are placed in a pocket when finished | They are lit in the hands of small children
exam: Ascertain the principal function of smoke alarms in the home. | They provide the minutes needed for escape | They extinguish fires automatically | They prevent fires from starting | They summon the fire brigade by themselves
